Binary Search Tree in C++

This program implements a menu-driven Binary Search Tree in C++. It allows the user to insert unique integer values and delete existing nodes from the tree. During deletion, the program first searches for the value to verify its existence and then removes it by handling leaf nodes, nodes with one child, and nodes with two children using the inorder successor. Note that the menu runs in an infinite loop and requires manual termination.

Time Complexity
The time complexity for insertion, deletion, and searching is O(h), where h is the height of the tree. In the average case for a balanced tree, the time complexity is O(log n). In the worst case, when the tree becomes skewed, the time complexity is O(n). The auxiliary space complexity is O(h) due to the recursive function call stack.

Sample Output

BINARY SEARCH TREE

1. Insert
2. Delete
Enter your choice: 1
Enter value to insert: 50
Value inserted successfully.

BINARY SEARCH TREE

1. Insert
2. Delete
Enter your choice: 2
Enter value to delete: 30
Value deleted successfully.

BINARY SEARCH TREE

1. Insert
2. Delete
Enter your choice: 2
Enter value to delete: 99
Value not found in BST.
